===============================================================================
PART 2 — THE CHARACTER SPRITE ART
===============================================================================

The character sprites are not our work and are not licensed under Part 1.
They are pixel art from the Liberated Pixel Cup (LPC) community, assembled
from the Universal LPC Spritesheet Character Generator.

Which files this covers
-----------------------

  * art/sprites/*.png — the sprite sheets themselves.  At the time of writing
    that is art/sprites/people.png (the characters), art/sprites/world.png
    (floors, furniture and wall items), and every file in art/sprites/parts/,
    which are the same LPC character layers one component per file — a build
    and skin, eyes, legs, shoes, a top, a cardigan, hair, a beard — for the
    character creator to stack in the browser.  These are the files to take if
    you want the art; they are the art, in unencumbered form, and nothing has
    to be extracted from anything else to get at them.

  * art/CREDITS.md and art/LICENSE-OGA-BY-3.0.txt, which are the attribution
    and the licence text that OGA-BY 3.0 requires to travel with the art.

Which files this does NOT cover
-------------------------------

  * art/sprites/manifest.js, despite living in the same directory.  It contains
    no art: it is a list of rectangles, sizes and names, generated by
    tools/build-sprites.mjs, describing where in those PNGs each sprite sits.
    That description is our work and is Part 1 material.

  * art/sprites/cars.png.  That is Part 4: ShareAlike, and kept out of
    every Part 2 sheet for the reason assertOnePart() exists.

  * index.html and data/*.js.  The art is loaded from the PNGs above at
    runtime; no encoded copy of it is embedded in any of them.

Earlier releases of this game embedded the art as base64 inside index.html and
shipped a single art/sprites.png beside it.  Neither arrangement still exists.
If you are reading a copy of this licence that describes them, it is out of
date; the files named above are the ones that matter.

Licence
-------

OGA-BY 3.0 (the OpenGameArt.org Attribution License 3.0), with some
contributions additionally or alternatively available under CC0.

  Full legal code:  art/LICENSE-OGA-BY-3.0.txt (included with this project)
  Canonical URI:    https://static.opengameart.org/OGA-BY-3.0.txt

OGA-BY 3.0 is CC-BY 3.0 with the clause prohibiting technological measures
removed.  It requires attribution.  It has no ShareAlike or copyleft term, and
it does not require works that incorporate the art to adopt its licence
(section 4(a): "this does not require the Collection apart from the Work
itself to be made subject to the terms of this License").

Every layer used here was selected because it is offered under OGA-BY 3.0 or
CC0.  Many LPC assets are offered only under CC-BY-SA 3.0 and/or GPL 3.0; none
of those were used in the files THIS Part covers.  That choice is what keeps
this Part's obligation to attribution alone.

ShareAlike assets are used elsewhere in the project, each in a file of its own,
under its own terms.  They are Parts 3 and 4.  tools/build-sprites.mjs refuses to pack
ShareAlike art into a Part 2 sheet — assertOnePart() — because doing so would
make that sheet an Adaptation of a ShareAlike work and force every OGA-BY
contribution in it into a licence its artists did not choose.

Attribution
-----------

The artists, the assets used, and the source URLs are listed in art/CREDITS.md.
That file is generated from the LPC generator's own sheet definitions rather
than written by hand, because attributing dozens of layered contributions
correctly is not something to do from memory.

Changes were made
-----------------

Required by OGA-BY 3.0 section 3(b), and stated here plainly: the original
artwork has been modified.  Layers were composited into single character
sprites, recoloured using the LPC palette definitions, cropped to the walking
and standing frames only, and packed into a single atlas image.  No original
LPC sheet is reproduced here intact.

No endorsement
--------------

Attribution does not imply that any LPC contributor, OpenGameArt.org, or the
Liberated Pixel Cup endorses this game or is connected with it.


===============================================================================
PART 3 — THE SHAREALIKE SHEETS
===============================================================================

Four files, kept apart from Part 2 and from each other on purpose.

Which files this covers
-----------------------

  * art/sprites/sanitary.png — the wall tile and the toilet pan drawn in the
    building's washrooms.  Two rectangles, cut from the Public Toilet Tileset
    and packed into a sheet of their own.

  * art/sprites/wood.png — the bench the town sits on and the clock on the
    wall.  Two rectangles, cut from the layered form of [LPC] Wooden Furniture
    and packed into a sheet of their own.

  * art/sprites/frontage.png — the shop windows along the parade.  Four
    rectangles, cut from [LPC] Windows & Doors and packed into a sheet of their
    own.

  * art/sprites/roofs.png — the roofs of the town, seen from above.  Sixty-five
    rectangles: five corner-matched sets of thirteen tiles each, cut from
    [LPC] Roofs and packed into a sheet of their own.

    Each sheet holds ONE work by ONE set of artists.  They are four files
    rather than one for the same reason none of them is packed into world.png:
    a sheet is an Adaptation of everything in it, and two unrelated ShareAlike
    works sharing a file would make each an Adaptation of the other's art with
    no way to say afterwards which artist's term reached which pixel.

Which files this does NOT cover
-------------------------------

  * art/sprites/victorian.png and art/sprites/cars.png.  Those are
    Part 4 — also ShareAlike, and also not this.  CC-BY-SA 3.0 and CC-BY-SA 4.0
    are different licences; see Part 4 for what turns on the difference.

  * art/sprites/world.png, art/sprites/people.png, art/sprites/town.png,
    art/sprites/streets.png, art/sprites/faces.png and art/sprites/parts/.
    Those are Part 2 and contain none of this art.  The build refuses to mix
    them; see assertOnePart() in tools/build-sprites.mjs.

  * art/sprites/manifest.js, for the same reason it is excluded from Part 2:
    it is rectangles and names we generated, and contains no art.

Licence
-------

CC-BY-SA 3.0 (Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported).

  Canonical URI:  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/legalcode

Section 4(a) permits the URI to stand in place of a copy of the licence text,
which is why there is no local copy of it here as there is for OGA-BY 3.0.

All four works are offered under CC-BY-SA 3.0 or GPL 3.0 (or GPL 3.0+), at the
licensee's option; [LPC] Wooden Furniture offers CC-BY-SA 4.0 as well.  This
project takes CC-BY-SA 3.0 for all four.  GPL 3.0 is the worse of the options here: its
"preferred form for making modifications" would reach the build inputs, and
tools/ is deliberately not published.  CC-BY-SA 4.0 is declined only because
3.0 is the version this part already states and one version is simpler to
honour than two; nothing is lost by it, as the 4.0 offer is an addition to the
3.0 one rather than a replacement for it.

Why this file is separate
-------------------------

ShareAlike attaches to Adaptations of the work.  art/sprites/sanitary.png is an
Adaptation — the art has been cropped and repacked — so that file is offered
under CC-BY-SA 3.0, and anyone may take it, change it and redistribute it on
those terms.

The game is not an Adaptation of it.  It is a Collection that includes the file
whole and unmodified alongside independent works, and CC-BY-SA 3.0 section 4(a)
says a Collection need not be made subject to this licence.  That is the same
argument Part 2 already makes, quoting the same clause, for the OGA-BY sheets.

Had this art been packed into world.png or town.png, that sheet would have
become an Adaptation of a ShareAlike work and every OGA-BY contribution in it
would have been dragged into a licence its artists never chose.  Hence the
separate files, and hence the build-time assertion that keeps them separate.

Attribution
-----------

art/sprites/sanitary.png

  Original author:  Matthew Nash
  Source:           https://opengameart.org/content/public-toilet-tileset
  Title:            Public Toilet Tileset

art/sprites/wood.png

  Original authors: bluecarrot16, Baŝto, Lanea Zimmerman (Sharm),
                    William Thompson, Tuomo Untinen (Reemax),
                    Janna/Lilius/Jannax
  Source:           https://opengameart.org/content/lpc-wooden-furniture
  Title:            [LPC] Wooden Furniture

art/sprites/frontage.png

  Original authors: bluecarrot16, Lanea Zimmerman (Sharm),
                    Daniel Armstrong (HughSpectrum), Casper Nilsson, Anamaris,
                    Krusmira, Keith Karnage, Guido Bos, Talosaurus
  Source:           https://opengameart.org/content/lpc-windows-doors
  Title:            [LPC] Windows & Doors

art/sprites/roofs.png

  Original authors: bluecarrot16, Lanea Zimmerman (Sharm),
                    Michele Bucelli (Buch), Casper Nilsson, Xenodora,
                    keith karnage, NaRNeRZz, Talon (Talosaurus)
  Source:           https://opengameart.org/content/lpc-roofs
  Title:            [LPC] Roofs

Changes were made
-----------------

Stated plainly, as section 4(c) requires: the original artwork has been
modified.

For sanitary.png: two rectangles — one wall tile and one toilet pan — were cut
from the tileset and packed into a single indexed PNG.

For wood.png: two pieces were cut from the dark-wood layer of the submission's
layered form and packed into a single indexed PNG.  The bench was additionally
SHORTENED — taken in two thirty-two pixel halves and joined, dropping the
twenty-six unbroken pixels between them, so that it is two tiles long rather
than nearly three.  The clock is the case from one layer with the dial from
another stacked back onto it, which is how the submission ships it.

For frontage.png: four rectangles — the same two-tile shopfront window in four
of the submission's eleven frame colours — were cut from its single sheet and
packed into one indexed PNG.  Nothing was recoloured or redrawn.

For roofs.png: sixty-five rectangles were cut from the submission's single
2048x2048 sheet and packed into one indexed PNG.  They are five of its
corner-matched sets — the thirteen tiles of field, edge and corner that make up
one roof material — chosen for colour and taken whole.  Nothing was recoloured
or redrawn.  What the game draws ON TOP of them at run time (the weathering,
and the plant, tanks, rooflights, aerials and chimney stacks) is drawn in code
in engine/render.js and is no part of this art.

No original sheet is reproduced here intact.

Standing of this attribution
----------------------------

Recorded honestly because it should be re-checkable, and the two files do not
stand in quite the same place.

For sanitary.png, the author and the licence were reported by the project owner
from the source page named above.  They could not be verified from inside the
build environment at the time, which could not reach opengameart.org or
web.archive.org; the PNG carries no tEXt chunk, and the name does not appear in
the official Liberated Pixel Cup asset repository.

For wood.png, frontage.png and roofs.png, the authors and the licences were
read off the source pages and off the CREDITS-*.txt each submission ships
beside its art, and they are written into tools/sheets/wood.mjs,
tools/sheets/frontage.mjs and tools/sheets/roofs.mjs where
they can be compared against those pages by anyone who opens them.  What the build re-checks is not the page but the
BYTES: the sheet pins the sha256 of the archive it was cut from and refuses to
build if the download no longer matches, so a re-upload under different terms
stops the build rather than passing through it.  That is a guarantee about the
art not having changed, not about the page having been read correctly; the
reading is still a human's, and if it is wrong it should be corrected.

The same notes are carried in art/CREDITS.md.

If this attribution is wrong, it should be corrected rather than removed: the
art is licensed, and the obligation is to credit the right person.

No endorsement
--------------

Attribution does not imply that Matthew Nash, bluecarrot16, any other artist
named above, OpenGameArt.org, or the Liberated Pixel Cup endorses this game or
is connected with it.


===============================================================================
PART 4 — THE CC-BY-SA 4.0 SHEETS
===============================================================================

Two files, kept apart from Parts 2 and 3 on purpose — from Part 3 as well as
from Part 2, which is the only thing here that is new.

Two files rather than one, and they are also kept apart from EACH OTHER: they
are two independent works from two different authors that happen to be offered
on the same terms, so each carries its own attribution block below.  Sharing a
part is not sharing a sheet, and nothing is packed into either of them.

Which files this covers
-----------------------

  * art/sprites/victorian.png — the painted iron bench, the pillar box, the
    cast-iron litter bin and the public clock standing in the old town.  Four
    rectangles, cut from the market and street sheets of one OpenGameArt
    submission and packed into a sheet of their own.

  * art/sprites/cars.png — the vehicles: thirty top-down cars, vans,
    buses and emergency vehicles on one sheet.  Reproduced WHOLE and
    byte-for-byte as supplied; four of the thirty are drawn by the game.

Which files this does NOT cover
-------------------------------

  * art/sprites/sanitary.png, art/sprites/wood.png, art/sprites/frontage.png
    and art/sprites/roofs.png.  Those are Part 3 and contain none of this art.
    ShareAlike does not make two works one work — which is also why the two
    sheets this part covers are two sheets and not one.

  * art/sprites/world.png, art/sprites/people.png, art/sprites/town.png,
    art/sprites/streets.png, art/sprites/faces.png and art/sprites/parts/.
    Those are Part 2.  The build refuses to mix any of these; see
    assertOnePart() in tools/build-sprites.mjs.

  * art/sprites/manifest.js, for the reason it is excluded from Parts 2 and 3:
    it is rectangles and names we generated, and contains no art.

Licence
-------

CC-BY-SA 4.0 (Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International).

  Canonical URI:  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode

Section 3(a)(2) permits the URI to stand in place of a copy of the licence
text, which is why there is no local copy of it here as there is for
OGA-BY 3.0.

This work is offered under CC-BY-SA 4.0 and under nothing else.  There is no
3.0 option to take and no GPL option to decline, which is the whole reason
this part exists: the alternative to a fourth part was not using the art.

Why this file is separate from Part 3
-------------------------------------

Both are ShareAlike, and it would be convenient to call them one thing.  They
are not one thing.

  * Relicensing.  4.0 section 3(b)(1) lets an Adaptation go out under a later
    version of the same licence; 3.0 section 4(b) does not say that of 4.0,
    because 4.0 did not exist when 3.0 was written.  A section that told a
    downstream user which versions they may use would have to give two
    different answers.

  * Attribution and notice.  3.0 section 4(c) and 4.0 section 3(a) require
    overlapping but differently-worded things, and 4.0 adds a 30-day cure
    period for a breach (section 6(b)) that 3.0 has no equivalent of.

  * Compatibility runs one way.  A 3.0 work may be adapted into a 4.0 one; the
    reverse is not available.  Merging the two sheets would therefore quietly
    put the Part 3 art under 4.0 — a relicensing this project has no business
    performing on somebody else's work, in a file they never agreed to be
    packed into.

So: two ShareAlike parts, two sheets, and a build that refuses to put a crop
from one anywhere near the other.  LICENCES_BY_PART in tools/lib/source.mjs is
where a sheet's part is checked against what it actually contains.

Why this file is separate from Part 2
-------------------------------------

The same argument Part 3 makes, unchanged: art/sprites/town.png is OGA-BY art,
and packing a ShareAlike crop into it would make that whole sheet an Adaptation
of a ShareAlike work and drag every OGA-BY contribution in it into a licence
its artists never chose.

The game is not an Adaptation of this file.  It is a Collection that includes
it whole and unmodified alongside independent works, and CC-BY-SA 4.0 section
1(f) read with section 4 says a Collection need not be made subject to this
licence.  That is the same argument Parts 2 and 3 already make under the
equivalent clauses of their own licences.

Attribution — art/sprites/victorian.png
---------------------------------------

  Original authors:  bluecarrot16, Casper Nilsson, Lanea Zimmerman (Sharm),
                     William Thompson, mold, AntumDeluge, Curt,
                     Daniel Eddeland (daneeklu), caeles, George Bailey,
                     Jetrel, Yar, Guido Bos, Ivan Voirol (Silver IV),
                     Stephen Challener (Redshrike), the Open Surge team,
                     Arthur Carvalho, Guilherme Vieira (n2liquid), NaRNeRZz,
                     Diarandor, Armando Montero (ArMM1998),
                     Tuomo Untinen (Reemax), Buch
  Source:            https://opengameart.org/content/lpc-victorian-town-decorations
  Title:             [LPC] Victorian Town Decorations

That is the list the submission's own CREDITS-decorations-victorian.txt gives,
in its own order, and it is reproduced in full because that file says it must
be.  The submission is itself a compilation of earlier LPC works, each named
with its own author and terms inside that credits file; anyone redistributing
art/sprites/victorian.png should carry it, and art/CREDITS.md does.

Changes were made — art/sprites/victorian.png
---------------------------------------------

Stated plainly, as section 3(a)(1)(B) requires: the original artwork has been
modified.  Four rectangles — a bench, a pillar box, a litter bin and a clock on
a post — were cut from two of the submission's three sheets and packed into a
single indexed PNG.  No original sheet is reproduced here intact.

Attribution — art/sprites/cars.png
----------------------------------

  Original author:   tokka
  Source:            https://tokka.itch.io/top-down-car
  Title:             Top Down Cars Sprite Pack — Basic Set

No changes were made — art/sprites/cars.png
-------------------------------------------

Stated plainly, because section 3(a)(1)(B) only requires a statement where
there are modifications and it is worth being clear that there are none: this
sheet is the file as supplied, byte for byte.  Nothing was cropped out of it,
nothing was repacked, and no part of it was re-encoded.

The game draws four of its thirty vehicles, and recolours three of those four
at runtime so that a car's paint can come from the game's own data rather than
from the sheet.  That recolouring happens on a canvas while the game is
running; it is not distributed, and no modified copy of this art exists in this
repository or in anything released from it.  What is distributed is the file
above, whole, alongside independent works — which is the Collection this part's
"Why this file is separate from Part 2" section describes.

Standing of this attribution — art/sprites/victorian.png
--------------------------------------------------------

The authors and the licence above were read off the source page named above and
off the CREDITS-decorations-victorian.txt the submission ships inside its own
archive, and they are written into tools/sheets/victorian.mjs where they can be
compared against that page by anyone who opens it.

What the build re-checks is not the page but the BYTES: the sheet pins the
sha256 of the archive it was cut from and refuses to build if the download no
longer matches, so a re-upload under different terms stops the build rather
than passing through it.  That is a guarantee about the art not having changed,
not about the page having been read correctly; the reading is still a human's,
and if it is wrong it should be corrected rather than removed.

The same note is carried in art/CREDITS.md.

Standing of this attribution — art/sprites/cars.png
---------------------------------------------------

Weaker than the one above it, and said plainly rather than glossed over.

This sheet did not arrive through tools/sheets/, so there is no pinned archive
to re-download and no upstream credits file to re-parse.  It was uploaded
straight into art/sprites/, and it carries no PNG metadata of any kind — no
tEXt chunk, no author, no licence.  The source page named above is refused by
the network the build and its sessions run behind, so the author and the
licence could not be read off it from in here.  Both were REPORTED BY THE
PROJECT OWNER from that page.

What is checked mechanically is the same thing Part 3's sheet checks and no
more: tools/lib/pinned.mjs records the sha256 of these bytes and the build
stops if they ever change on disk.  That is a guarantee that the art has not
moved underneath the attribution, not that the attribution is right.

If it is wrong it should be corrected rather than removed — the art is
licensed, and the obligation is to credit the right person.  The same note is
carried in art/CREDITS.md.

No endorsement
--------------

Attribution does not imply that tokka, any artist named above, itch.io,
OpenGameArt.org, or the Liberated Pixel Cup endorses this game or is connected
with it.


